# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prices
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario The price list sets the last price of each security
Description The price list is a comma-separated oddity in the middle of a
Description field-per-line format. Only the latest price per security is used:
Description the earlier ones would each post a revaluation, and twenty years of
Description daily prices would bury the register.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given holdings are : Holdings
| Account                | Symbol | Shares | Price    | PriceAsOf  |
| Assets:Brokerage:VTSAX | VTSAX  | 100    | 100.0000 | 2024-02-01 |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Prices
"VTSAX",105.00,"3/29'24"
^
"VTSAX",110.00,"6/28'24"
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account                     | Amount   |
| T1  | 2024-06-28 | Assets:Brokerage:VTSAX      | 1000.00  |
| T1  | 2024-06-28 | Equity:Market Value Changes | -1000.00 |
Then holdings are : Holdings
| Account                | Symbol | Shares | Price    | PriceAsOf  |
| Assets:Brokerage:VTSAX | VTSAX  | 100    | 110.0000 | 2024-06-28 |
Then migration notes are : MigrationNote
| Kind          | Detail                                            |
| PricesSkipped | 1 earlier price for VTSAX was not used            |

BusinessRule How a price list line is read
Description Quoted symbol, bare price, quoted date, and the date uses the same
Description apostrophe century marker as everywhere else.
Examples: PriceLine
| Line                        | Symbol | Price    | AsOf       | Valid |
| "VTSAX",110.00,"6/28'24"    | VTSAX  | 110.0000 | 2024-06-28 | true  |
| "BRK.B",405.1234,"1/14'22"  | BRK.B  | 405.1234 | 2022-01-14 | true  |
| "VTSAX",110,"6/28'24"       | VTSAX  | 110.0000 | 2024-06-28 | true  |
| "VTSAX",,"6/28'24"          | VTSAX  | 0        | 2024-06-28 | false |
| "VTSAX",110.00              | VTSAX  | 110.0000 | 2024-01-01 | false |

Attributes PriceLine
| Attribute | Type   | Default    |
| Line      | String |            |
| Symbol    | Symbol |            |
| Price     | Price  | 0          |
| AsOf      | Date   | 2024-01-01 |
| Valid     | Boolean | true      |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Memorized transactions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Memorized transactions become payee rules
Description Quicken remembers a payee and the category it takes, which is what
Description a payee rule is. Bringing them over means the categorising that was
Description done over twenty years is not done again. See Payees.
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Memorized
KC
PHarris Teeter
LGroceries
T-45.00
^
KC
PShell
LAuto:Fuel
T-52.00
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then payee rules created are : PayeeRules
| Pattern       | MatchType | Payee         | Category           | Enabled |
| Harris Teeter | Contains  | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | true    |
| Shell         | Contains  | Shell         | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | true    |
# The remembered amount is not brought over. A rule that filled in an amount
# from 2009 would do more harm than good.

Scenario A memorized split becomes a remembered split rule
Description The mortgage split that Payees exists to handle, arriving ready
Description made.
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Memorized
KC
PFirst National
SHome:Interest
$-820.00
SMortgage
$-430.00
SHome:Escrow
$-250.00
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then payee split rules created are : PayeeSplitRule Vertical
| Pattern   | First National |
| MatchType | Contains       |
| Payee     | First National |
| Shape     | Amounts        |
Then remembered split lines created are : RememberedSplitLine
| Category               | Amount | Memo |
| Expenses:Home:Interest | 820.00 | none |
| Expenses:Mortgage      | 430.00 | none |
| Expenses:Home:Escrow   | 250.00 | none |
# Mortgage was not in the category list and the sign made it an expense. It is
# a liability account in the book being built, so this is one of the lines the
# migration report is for.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The run as a whole
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule In what order a QIF file is read
Description Four passes, because a transaction cannot be placed until the
Description accounts and categories it names are known.
Examples: MigrationPass
| Pass | Reads                                     | Why                                      |
| 1    | Account, category, class and security lists | A transaction names these               |
| 2    | Every transaction record, with its account | Nothing is created yet; halves are paired |
| 3    | Transactions, splits and transfers          | In date order, so the register reads right |
| 4    | Price list, then statement balance checks   | Revaluation is last, on final holdings  |

Attributes MigrationPass
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Pass      | Integer | 0       |
| Reads     | String  |         |
| Why       | String  |         |

Scenario Nothing is written until the whole file has been read without error
Description A migration that half succeeded would leave a book that is neither
Description the old data nor the new, and no way to tell which transactions had
Description arrived. It is all or nothing.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D01/15/2024
T-45.00
PHarris Teeter
^
D99/99/9999
T-12.00
PBad Record
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                        |
| Line 7: 99/99/9999 is not a date; nothing was imported        |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date | Account | Amount |
# No rows: the good record was not written either.

Scenario A migration report is produced whether or not anything was odd
Description The report is the deliverable of a migration, not a by-product. It
Description is what makes it possible to say the twenty years came over, and
Description to find the handful of things that did not.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given the file contains
"""
!Option:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
/12/31/2024
$955.00
^
NSavings
TBank
^
!Clear:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
^
!Type:Bank
D01/01/2005
T2500.00
POpening Balance
L[Checking]
^
D01/15/2024
T-45.00
PHarris Teeter
LGroceries
^
D03/01/2024
T-1500.00
PTransfer to savings
L[Savings]
^
!Account
NSavings
TBank
^
!Type:Bank
D03/01/2024
T1500.00
PTransfer from checking
L[Checking]
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then migration report is : MigrationReport Vertical
| AccountsCreated   | 3       |
| CategoriesCreated | 2       |
| Transactions      | 3       |
| Postings          | 6       |
| TransfersPaired   | 1       |
| Unsupported       | 0       |
| Notes             | 0       |
| Balanced          | true    |
Then balance checks are : LedgerBalanceResult Vertical
| ClearedBalance | 955.00 |
| StatedBal      | 955.00 |
| Difference     | 0.00   |
| Agrees         | true   |
# Three accounts: Assets:Checking, Assets:Savings and Equity:Opening Balances.
# Two categories: Expenses:Groceries and the Expenses placeholder above it.

Attributes MigrationReport
| Attribute         | Type    | Default |
| AccountsCreated   | Integer | 0       |
| CategoriesCreated | Integer | 0       |
| Transactions      | Integer | 0       |
| Postings          | Integer | 0       |
| TransfersPaired   | Integer | 0       |
| Unsupported       | Integer | 0       |
| Notes             | Integer | 0       |
| Balanced          | Boolean | false   |

Scenario Every transaction in the book balances after the migration
